A||||14||artherd||Should the AC Compressor turn on when I use Defrost?||||1||
Z||000000||artherd||09-12-2001||03:43 AM||||Well? ;P)<P>I didn't think the defrost air activated the AC, but I suppose it might.  Never feels cold though, and the AC normally works.<P><BR>AC Compressor clutch operates intermittantly, as it should (it's not shorted on or anything.)<P><BR>Can anybody else feel their AC clutch tic on and off?  My car darned well lurches forward and back, and there's a very noticable power loss when it's on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leper||09-12-2001||04:22 AM||||Yes<BR>I hear that it's supposed to keep the compressor from locking up from underuse. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||09-12-2001||05:33 AM||||I was under the impression that using A/C with the defrost (that's why the comp kicks on?) will blow <B>dry</B> air on the windshield, helping to clear it.  I have no idea how defrost works on a car with no A/C, though.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||09-12-2001||07:19 AM||||If you have a/c, the compressor comes on with defrost, if you turn the heat on it blows "dried" warm air.  If you don't have a/c, defrost blows air, turn the heat on, it blows warm air.<P>In the winter, the defrost with a/c pumps the refrigerant and lubricates all the "O" rings in the fittings.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spiff||09-12-2001||12:33 PM||||I always thought the AC turned on with the de-froster to ack like a de-humidifier drying the air and windshield quicker. At least that's what I have been told. On another note, my AC compressor makes a screeching sound when it on, so I don't use AC anymore. Does anyone know if the AC clutch can be replaced or do I need to replace the compressor. It's on an 88 Formula 5 speed.||||reg||7
Z||000005||StuGood||09-12-2001||12:46 PM||||What everyone just said...<P>Yes, A/C compressor comes on with defrost, when temperature is below 40 degrees or so.  It dehumidifies, and it works well - effectively defrosts the windshield (even before the engine warms up).  Also helps the seals' longevity.<P>A/C clutch <B>is</B> a replaceable item, if it's slipping.  If there's squealing when the compressor comes on, probably should first check the belt.    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-12-2001||07:39 PM||||In Fiero's case, AC comes on in defrost regardless of temperature. Good thing too. The time I usually need dehumdification most is when it's warm out and raining... Man these little cars can fog up quick.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnF||09-12-2001||07:41 PM||||Yes! Yes! Yes!  The A/C is working properly.  Leave it alone.  Defrosted (dehudified) air dries out the inside of the car much better and faster than non-dehumidified.  Clears out all glass parts whether or not air is directed to them.||||reg||10
Z||000008||artherd||09-13-2001||06:28 AM||||Hrm, nifty and clever!   Amazed I never noticed this before (and I swear somehow the air is never cold...  wait... is that in the silver car?)   The dehumidification makes perfect sense, but the added bonous of lubing the AC parts in the winter is neat!<P>My Silver formula didn't have AC, so of course it wouldn't blow cold under defrost silly!!  aargh, ben go sleepy now :S)<P>Amazing how much I learn on this fourm every day, thanks guys!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||Robert||09-13-2001||07:23 PM||||A long time ago (1975?) in a far off place (Lake Tahoe, Ca., winter) an engineer friend of mine told me to turn on the air conditioner to clear up the condensation on the inside of the window. His reason for doing it is different than what I’ve heard here and other places.  He said the reason it would clear up the window is that it brings the glass temperature down to equal the outside air temperature, so the windows don’t fog.  Made since to me, as I remembered the beer mugs fogging up as soon as you bring them out of the freezer.  <BR>Any thoughts?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000010||theogre||09-13-2001||07:51 PM||||You sort of have it right... The AC changes the Dew Point of everything. I'm not going to actually try to explain Dew Point... It's a function of temperature and humidity. (Try AskJeeves.com or howstuffworks.com for more on Dew Point.) <P>The general idea is that the glass has to be above Dew Point or water condenses on it. <P>When you turn on the AC you drop the temperature of the air and condense out allot of water as it passes thru the evaporator. If the air entering the car is colder than the glass then you get the same effect as heating the glass. Plus you pump in dryer air so there is less humidity to condense. End result is clear glass. This works quite well in warm wet climates.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||ichifiero||09-14-2001||10:17 PM||||I have noticed that I hear the compressor clicking on and of any time the fan is turned on (whether it be defrost, vent, or heater).  I nedd to make sure that it is "off" when first starting my car int he morning since it will load up the motor to much making it difficult to idle. I thought that there was something wrong with the car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||09-14-2001||11:44 PM||||The only two positions where the AC compressor is off are heat and vent.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||ichifiero||09-15-2001||02:46 PM||||If the AC shold not be on in the vent or heat mode, does anyone know how I can find out what the problem might be. Obbiously my gas milage suffers if I just fun the fan in vent mode.<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||09-15-2001||05:08 PM||||If you don't want anything on.... there is an off button...||||reg||1
